WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.611 --> 00:00:03.194
(gentle music)

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.710
- Welcome for another episode
of the "Addy Hour" this week,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:11.240
I'm really excited about our
conversation about mental,

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.670
physical, and spiritual
health with Dr. R. Kweku Smith

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.200
and Pastor Shino Prater.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.410
And in a lot of ways
this really encapsulates

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.350
what we are about on this podcast,

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.710
really thinking about
all aspects of our health

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.950
and trying to think about
things in a holistic

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.590
and integrated manner.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:27.440
So both these gentlemen,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.650
I'm really excited to
have them on the podcast

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.670
because of who they are and
because both them really

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.070
epitomize this in their
investment in the community

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.900
in so many different ways.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.000
So our first guest I'd like
to introduce to everyone

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.350
is Shino Prater, who I
know is Pastor Shino.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.740
Pastor Shino is married
to Anne Marie Prater,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.900
and also the father of three children.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.480
He's a former football standout

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.660
who played his college ball at Penn State.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.130
He also played in the NFL
for the Tennessee Titans

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.200
and played in the Canadian Football League

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.420
for the Hamilton Tiger, the Tiger Cats.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.970
So obviously he had a lot of
success on the football field

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:04.350
and was able to make an
impact in that sphere.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.470
But even while he was
playing professional ball,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.130
also really invested in communities

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.430
in a lot of different ways.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.390
Something that I know is
near and dear to his heart

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:12.850
and something that lots of people

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.060
are appreciative to him for.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.830
Right now he's an associate pastor

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.260
at Every Nation Church in New York City.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.090
He's also an assistant
chaplain for the New York Jets,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.340
and he's formerly served as a youth pastor

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.710
and the campus pastor.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.850
So again, someone who's really
been invested in communities

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:28.980
in a lot of different ways,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.610
and I know someone who's
invested in whole health

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:32.880
and wholeness for our community.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.280
So definitely grateful
to welcome Pastor Shino

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.000
to the program today,
thanks for being here.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.210
- Thank you for having me, it's an honor.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.070
Our next guest is Dr. R. Kweku Smith,

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.040
who is the president and CEO

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.890
of Black Hugh Smith Psychological
Consultative Services.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:52.190
Dr. Smith earned his degree,
his PhD in urban education

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.630
with an emphasis in school psychology.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.910
And he's someone throughout his career

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.370
who's also really just invested

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.260
in so many different communities.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.620
So just to highlight a few,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.810
he has experience in working and teaching

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.000
in the public school system,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.280
both the elementary school
level and the high school level.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.740
He's also been involved in
several other aspects as well.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.330
He's worked in the Trauma Center

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.610
at the Children's Hospital of Wisconsin,

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.740
and has also been involved
Department Of Corrections,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.410
working both with male
and female offenders.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.420
And he's also worked in organizations

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.290
on topics like workplace
performance, diversity, inclusion,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.030
and mental health.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.930
He's previously served as a psychologist

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:29.990
for the Milwaukee Bucks.

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.430
Definitely, we have to give
a shout out to the Bucks

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.310
with the way they're
playing in the playoffs,

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.310
with their deep runs so far.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.310
But he's also been involved
with the NFL as well,

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.400
working with lots of different NFL teams.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.700
He serves on the clinical
advisory team as well.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.830
And again, someone who is
an activist in many ways,

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.080
and really deeply
invested in the community

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.140
around all aspects related to
mental health and wellness.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.650
So grateful to be able to
welcome Dr. Kweku Smith

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.090
to the program today,
thanks for being here.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.640
- And thanks for having
me, it's an absolute honor.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.380
- Well, again, I'm
grateful to both of you.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.820
It's definitely a mutual honor.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.340
I'm just so grateful for
the work that you've done

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.460
and for your willingness to share

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.930
some of that work with our listeners,

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.870
even as we are in this state
of emerging from the pandemic.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.950
And then as our listeners
know, as my listeners know,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.740
we often just like to check in with people

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.470
and see where they are.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.610
Sometimes people I know can
feel like it's burdensome

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.220
to continue to talk about those things,

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.550
but I think it's important for
us just to be real and honest

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.300
with how we're all doing
on a day-to-day basis.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.098
So Dr. Smith, if we could start with you,

00:03:28.098 --> 00:03:29.480
if you'd just be willing
to give us a little bit

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.880
of how you're doing these days,

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.830
as we all are working through these things

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.243
as a community together.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.624
- Yeah, so making sure I
understand your question right,

00:03:37.624 --> 00:03:40.610
just how am I doing
personally during these days?

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.210
How am I navigating
during the COVID season?

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.939
And I think that's an excellent question

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:46.860
because there's always a question to say,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.200
who helps the helpers?

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.720
Sometimes you're so busy giving.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.260
And I know as a pastor,
when I look at Pastor Shino,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.240
I'm thinking, wow, what he has
to give to his congregation

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:59.350
on a daily basis and how
that's amplified here.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.710
And though I would never
equate myself to like a pastor,

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.280
being in a helping circle,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.450
you are one who are
continually in this vessel.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.736
You're pouring out,

00:04:07.736 --> 00:04:10.400
and so the other sort of
question is how do I remain

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.600
feel so I can make sure
I can give to others?

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:13.433
And the honest truth,

00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:15.760
this has been some highs
and there's been some lows.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.520
I think there's a natural reciprocity

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.810
of refilling your cup by giving to others.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.750
This that when I help
someone else it's a natural

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.200
either from a biological
neurological standpoint,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.050
we talk about serotonin
and dopamine levels,

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.450
those things being increased
when you do something good.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.530
But on a more like just intrinsic level,

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.170
I think when we do something good

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.890
just in our heart it helps us.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.110
But then it becomes that tipping point

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.410
where you're giving and you're
giving and you're giving

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.990
and until where you're exhausted.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.210
And what I'll tell you is what's been

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.710
one of the most difficult things for me,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.570
is to make sure that not
only do I do well at work,

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.660
but what suffered the most was my home.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.490
I could go to work and I
could get some things done.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.040
Well, when I was going
home, I was finding myself,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.340
giving my wife, giving my
children the absolute worst.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.630
You know, I'm tired physically,
I'm tired emotionally,

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.490
quicker to snap and things of that nature.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.050
And so when you look
in the mirror and say,

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:10.100
wow, if I'm helping other
people, if I'm giving strategies,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.360
and if I can't employ these,
maybe they're not working

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.920
or why aren't I'm doing

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.320
what I'm actually giving
other people's to do.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.177
And so to that point, what I decided to do

00:05:18.177 --> 00:05:20.970
was to make a conscious
effort, being very intentional

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.501
about what was I going to
do at the start in my day.

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:24.460
And that the start of my day,

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.470
I was gonna give honor to my creator,

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:28.350
give honor to the ancestors,
those who laid the path.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.150
But then the next was those people

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.590
who were right in front of me.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:32.940
How was I gonna be a better dad?

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.650
How was I gonna be a better husband?

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.920
So asking questions.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.550
And this was probably the most humbling,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.300
but the best thing I could've done.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.847
I asked my daughter, I said,

00:05:41.847 --> 00:05:43.730
"Write me out a list of five things

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.980
that you would like for
me to improve upon."

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.860
And, oh, she she's 10 at the time.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:50.737
And she was like,

00:05:50.737 --> 00:05:52.850
"Well, maybe you can not
compare us to other people,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.350
but maybe you can not holler us,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:56.040
but maybe you could be
home a little more."

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.470
And you see things and it's very humbling,

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.890
but I was so happy that
she could be honest.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:02.810
And so what did I do?

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.010
I started to make sure that
I was gonna spend more time.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.110
I was gonna be more conscious

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:09.070
about how I spoke to my family,
I was gonna do those things.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:12.370
And in that made me feel
a lot better, Dr. Addy,

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.760
because then what I started to see

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.520
is the things I was
telling other people to do,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.430
if I implemented myself,
these things really work.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.037
So we're getting more confident in it,

00:06:20.037 --> 00:06:22.330
but it was only because of these falls

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:23.980
that I was able to be able to see it.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.650
But it was being humbled
enough to hear it,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.550
then being brave enough to ask for help

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.240
and then being courageous enough

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.327
to try to seek out different things.

00:06:31.327 --> 00:06:33.090
And so we did some of those things,

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.650
we started building in our
house, we started doing projects,

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.240
things that gave us a purpose.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.880
We bought some puppies, I
don't advise you buy two.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.500
I kind of went overboard with that,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.010
but what we wanted to do
though in this concept,

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.310
is it's to say, what can
we do to inject love,

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.540
to inject something different,
because we are being infused

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.640
with something different
from a negative standpoint,

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.210
so how could we counter that?

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:54.400
And through that time point,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.920
there's still some ebbs and flows,

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.430
but what I've recognized
is it's a daily struggle.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.220
And what I have to do is look
at myself on a daily basis

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.990
and say, what were my goals for the day?

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.520
It didn't at the end of
that day, start to evaluate,

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.880
did I accomplish these things?

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.460
If so, why did I, if not, why didn't I?

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.990
And if I didn't, what are the
things I can do to do better?

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.490
So even as we go through it,

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:16.310
I think it's still not a
thing that's in process.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.040
It's a very fluid situation.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.593
So as you talk about what did we do?

00:07:19.593 --> 00:07:22.627
I would say, what am I
doing to try to make sure

00:07:22.627 --> 00:07:24.787
that I don't go too far below again,

00:07:24.787 --> 00:07:28.180
to try to stay even keel
so I can be good at home,

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.150
in the community and
then also with myself?

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.060
- Mm, that's so good, that's so good.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.310
And I appreciate that level of
honesty and just the bravery,

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.700
I think in your self-reflection too.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.840
The fact that you asked
your 10 year old daughter,

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.180
I think that takes some bravery

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.446
because you're gonna get an honest answer.

00:07:42.446 --> 00:07:43.410
(all laughing)

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:44.700
You obviously did.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.034
And just being willing
to take that step too,

00:07:47.034 --> 00:07:48.450
because I think I've heard
people say those things

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:49.360
individually too, like,

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.520
oh, wow, I feel like I'm on thin ice.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.030
I'm snapping a lot quicker than I used to.

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:55.370
And just to be able to
have that self-reflection

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.210
take a step back and then be
willing to let your daughter

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:00.120
take that step and to walk with her,

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.070
and I think that goes such a long way.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.340
I think there's a lot that
we can all pull from that.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.680
So appreciate you sharing
with that level of honesty.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.000
I also love to know the
fact that you're applying

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.610
what you preach too.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:12.460
I think that's easy for us,

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.180
I'd like Pastor Shino jumping in too,

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.690
but for us who are in
the business of healing

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.850
in any sort of type of
way, that sometimes it can,

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:21.100
I know personally it can be harder

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.150
to kind of incorporate
that in my own life.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:24.400
Even if I'm telling my students that,

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.630
even if I'm giving talks and
sharing that with others,

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:27.640
but when it really comes down

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.730
to walking through it yourself.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:32.093
So I appreciate you sharing
that journey with us as well.

00:08:35.193 --> 00:08:36.890
- Yeah, Pastor Shino, what about you?

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.060
- So for me, I mean,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.450
I have to echo everything the doctor said,

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:44.620
is because it is hard to give
when you're not receiving.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:48.200
And so I had to early on
make just a mental note

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.700
that this is a marathon and not a sprint.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.820
And I had to pace myself

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.140
because it was so much coming at me.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.830
You know, phone, my phone
rang and it was nothing good.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:08:59.970
It was nothing good.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:04.490
And so it was, I got
this, contracted COVID,

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:07.950
such and such is in the
hospital, they may not make it.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.304
So it was a lot of just
stuff coming at you.

00:09:10.304 --> 00:09:15.304
People losing jobs, people
moving, we had to pivot,

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:18.190
because of what was going on,
the church wasn't meeting,

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:19.920
so we had to go online.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:24.140
So it was a lot of stuff
to try to mobilize people.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.780
And not only people like your congregation

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.470
and keep them full of faith

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:33.470
and keep them pointed towards
the Lord and lean in to Him.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.680
But just like doctor said, I
had to take care of my home.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:42.270
And so one of the things that I had to do,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:45.500
was I had to make sure
before I did anything,

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.060
is check in with my wife and
checking with my children

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:53.360
because my oldest, she was
a way she was in college.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:54.677
It was her senior year.

00:09:54.677 --> 00:09:57.380
And of course, you know, she's just sad

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.170
because, "Hey, this is my last year,

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.040
this is how it's going to go down."

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.130
And they were half and half in school.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.897
And so I'm trying to
encourage my 22 year old,

00:10:05.897 --> 00:10:08.370
"Don't quit, don't give
up, you made it this far,

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:09.900
you're gonna graduate."

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.920
Then my middle daughter, she
enrolled in college in the city

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.380
and they're not having college.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:17.370
So her first year was online.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.550
So she's upset, you know,
"Dad, why's this going on?

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:23.360
You know, this is my
first year of college."

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:25.456
So she's going through it.

00:10:25.456 --> 00:10:30.220
And my son being 17 years
old, wanting to play sports

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.300
and not being able to see his friends.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.240
And so that was a difficult time.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:36.660
And so just like Doc
was saying, it was like,

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:39.310
man, I can't be out there
trying to save the world

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:40.950
and I'm not taking care of my own home,

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.930
I'm not taking care of my space.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:45.470
And so to check myself, I had to make sure

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.720
that I was putting in as
much as I could put in

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.650
by reading my scriptures,
reading the Word of God,

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.890
spending time with the Lord.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:57.150
Not only that, but listening to podcasts.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:00.930
Being able to not take a call
every time something came in,

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:02.660
because some of these calls

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.980
that were coming in were so taxing.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.930
You wanna run to help, but
man, you can't do anything,

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:09.880
you can't do what you need to do.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:13.129
You know, spending time
with you, Doc, Dr. Addy,

00:11:13.129 --> 00:11:16.520
listening and to other doctors.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.200
And that was important
because doctors were saying,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.940
you need to rest, you need to hydrate,

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:23.620
you need to disconnect.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:24.730
You know, as a pastor,

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.390
you wanna think you can
save the world sometimes,

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:28.840
but you know, you gotta understand,

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.530
you gotta give stuff to over to God.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:33.450
You gotta give it to the Lord
and do the practical things

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:35.130
and take care of yourself.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:39.080
The other thing was
rest, sleep is important

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.160
because if you're not careful,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.860
you can go deep into
the night doing stuff.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.060
You you don't have any margins,

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.850
meetings going into 10:00, 11:00 o'clock

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.940
at night with people up on the phone,

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:54.387
and just having to do those things.

00:11:54.387 --> 00:11:57.730
And so for me, it was just slowing down

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:59.880
and saying look one day at a time

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:05.570
and rejoicing at every victory,

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.570
because there's something
about stopping and rejoicing

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:11.980
about the littlest things,
some of the smallest things,

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:14.870
bringing hope, bringing life into my home.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:17.320
And we celebrated that as a family

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.870
and I wanted my kids to
get in on that as well

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:21.820
because I wanted them to see

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:23.580
that there were better days ahead.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:27.187
And a lot of times we
overlook the littlest things.

00:12:27.187 --> 00:12:31.280
And I think for me, waking
up in the morning was like,

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:33.140
I'm gonna thank the Lord for that

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:35.923
because there were some
people that didn't wake up.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:38.590
Thank God that I still got a house,

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:42.150
that I still got food on my
table, clothes on my back,

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.520
just going through.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.680
And I began to tell everybody in my home,

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:49.230
you need to identify and start
thanking God for everything.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.130
There's anything you can think of,

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:54.260
because that's gonna bring
you hope that He's here

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.580
and He's gonna keep us moving forward.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:01.180
So just like Doc said, there
were some dark moments,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.740
doing funerals on Zoom

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.370
and not being able to weep
and cry with the people

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:07.440
when they're going through things

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.770
because of the isolation and
the separation, it was tough.

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:14.340
But the one thing that really I can say

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:17.183
that helped get me through was community.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.860
Just talking to people,
praying with people,

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:25.950
crying with people,
this being in community,

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:26.940
the best that we could,

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:30.160
even though with a physical
community at the time,

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.970
it was just the moments
of just being online

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:34.870
and seeing someone's face.

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:37.350
And for me, I'm wired a little different,

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:41.687
I think doc said it as
well, I like to help people.

00:13:41.687 --> 00:13:44.820
And so I went and we have
other churches around the world

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.200
and I got other pastor friends
that were going through it

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:49.150
way worse than we were,

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.830
prior to what was going
on here in New York.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:55.270
And I remember man, just saying,
"How can I stand with you,

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:57.980
what can I do for you
during this dark moment?"

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:01.060
Because it was bad over
in Europe at the time.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:04.160
And he was just saying, you
know, "Just pray with me, man,

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:09.160
cry with me, just stand,
let me talk as much as I can

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:10.687
about what's going on in my home

00:14:10.687 --> 00:14:12.560
and with my wife and my children,

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:14.910
because I feel like
I'm alone over a here."

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:17.530
And man, it was just a good moment

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:19.100
to see him get through it.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:21.960
And then he was with in our
dark moment as well over here.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.450
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:24.940
Well, appreciate it, definitely
appreciate you sharing

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:26.850
so honestly and powerfully as well.

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.230
I mean, just so many similar themes

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:29.063
for the two of you as well.

00:14:29.063 --> 00:14:31.760
Just that balance between
the importance of giving

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:32.810
and how that's good for us,

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:33.830
but then also knowing the balance

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.760
between taking time to step back

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.640
and make sure that we're still
present with our families,

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.380
making sure that we're
still present for ourselves

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:43.470
and taking care of our
own health and balance.

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:44.900
And sleep is one of the things

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:46.430
we'll be talking about
in a future podcast,

00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:49.320
just the effects of sleep on
mental health and vice versa.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:50.290
So I think that's so important

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:51.920
that you brought that up as well,

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.465
but so many themes that cut across

00:14:53.465 --> 00:14:56.750
what you both talked about,
just the importance of community

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:58.520
and the importance of serving,

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:01.210
and also the importance of perspective.

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:02.840
Pastor Shino you talked
about just having people

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.330
be thankful daily about the little things,

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.970
and I'm sure Dr. Smith, you
could talk about the effects

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:10.550
that it has for us
psychologically and on our brains.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:11.710
So I think there's so many topics

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:14.100
that we're highlighting here
that I think are critical.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.936
And I'm curious for both
of you, two-part question

00:15:16.936 --> 00:15:19.110
in the sense of how the
people in your communities

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:21.210
have been doing and what advice

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:22.820
you've been trying to give
people in the communities

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.370
where you work to really move through

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.220
and to get to a place
of healing and wholeness

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.500
in the midst of everything
we've been facing as a society.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:31.900
So Dr. Smith, if you wanna
jump in with that question

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:33.380
or those questions.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:34.980
- Yeah. So I'm kind of like in the middle

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:36.080
of two communities.

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.650
So I do a lot of work with
the Wisconsin Badgers.

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:42.870
So I'm working with student
athletes in Madison, Wisconsin,

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.570
but I'm based in Milwaukee, Wisconsin,

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.540
which parental leave
for the last five years

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:50.710
have been either in the
top five, two or three,

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.640
unfortunately is the worst
place for a black male to live.

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.150
So those are two completely
different populations.

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:58.190
So working with a student
athlete population

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:02.120
where troubles are significant,
it's completely different

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:03.510
than what I'm dealing with in the city.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:05.387
So and working with the
student athletes you say,

00:16:05.387 --> 00:16:08.040
"Hey, how do I navigate
you being an athlete

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:09.270
at a Power Five Conference

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:11.460
with all of the pressure
that goes along with that?

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:14.780
Now with the different
disadvantages that COVID bring,

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:16.440
as it relates to our schedule,

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:17.810
that the bubbles it has created,

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:20.780
decided at different pressures
and still being a student.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:21.730
And then just other things

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:23.300
that come with regular college life,

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:24.820
those issues that they have,

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:26.440
sometimes we see a small problems,

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:27.910
but those who are going through it,

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:29.470
is absolutely monumental

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:31.920
and trauma comes in different
forms in different fashion.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:33.550
So what I try to teach them

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:35.830
and even to the second community
which I'll speak on next,

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:38.394
is how do we maximize the moment of now?

00:16:38.394 --> 00:16:40.010
When we think about the past,

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:41.080
we're thinking about depression.

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:42.160
When we think about the future,

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:43.710
we're thinking about anxiety.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:45.100
So those things are going
to come, but we'll say,

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:47.660
how can I control the
moment that I'm in now?

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:49.790
Because the things that's
happened in the past,

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.530
those things have hurt me,
but I can't change them.

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:53.550
My thing is how do I try to equip myself

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.890
to be able to deal with them
no matter how difficult,

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:58.260
they are understanding that
I can get through whatever.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.580
And as it relates to that
anxiety for the future saying,

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:03.500
how do I not let that fear
of what's going to happen?

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.780
That may not even manifest
paralyze me from doing things now

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:09.560
that could actually benefit
me from what's going on.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:11.460
Now in Dillon, in Milwaukee, where I'm at,

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:14.540
where I think we ranked like either worse

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.520
or next to work on
every social determinate

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:19.570
in the United States, from
the cradle to the graze.

00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:22.290
Talking about premature
birth rate, low birth rate,

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:25.120
education, suspensions,
detention, unemployment,

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:27.510
under employment, health, STIs,

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:31.080
all the way to being in
the most incarcerated state

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:32.120
in the United States.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:36.060
And then on top of that,
having a higher death rate.

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:37.530
So when we look at these things, you say,

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:40.560
well, how do I work with people
who are dealing with COVID,

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.450
who are dealing with systemic racism,

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:43.420
who are dealing with things

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:44.640
where they have literal problems.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:46.370
Now, how am I going to eat?

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:48.670
My mom is in jail, my dad is dead.

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:50.230
Like, well, we have
these different problems.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:53.090
It's a little bit different of
a focus on what can you say?

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:56.340
So we're still goes back
to what can I do now?

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:57.550
What can I control?

00:17:57.550 --> 00:18:00.250
And what we have to do is
to be able to instill a hope

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:02.780
and a level of empowerment
inside of people to understand,

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:06.260
no matter how horrific our
tragic seemingly our situation,

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:08.770
that something beautiful
can still come from it

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.950
if we don't give up, if we keep pushing.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:12.480
No matter how desperate it may seem,

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:14.430
no matter how dark it may be at the time,

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:16.270
it's about how do we create that hope?

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:17.700
And then going back to what you say,

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:19.550
even in the midst of some of that trauma,

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:20.855
what are some of the
blessings that was there,

00:18:20.855 --> 00:18:21.688
what were some of the ways

00:18:21.688 --> 00:18:23.570
in which we could have been
caught in and that we weren't,

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:26.200
which gives us a light to
know that there is a reason,

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:27.750
there is a purpose for our being.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.440
And maybe we are going to be the spark

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.370
that makes it a lot
better for other people

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:33.450
who had to come the way we are coming up

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:34.910
or we'll come up that way.

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:37.493
So when you say like, what
population are you dealing with?

00:18:37.493 --> 00:18:39.400
It's so many different avenues you go,

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.170
depending on who you're talking to.

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:44.160
So when you're dealing with
people who their issues

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:46.230
are kind of like, seemingly not trauma,

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:48.830
but it is trauma to them,
you give them that hope

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.817
as far as where they're
going, what they can do.

00:18:50.817 --> 00:18:53.210
But when you're dealing with
people who are in the midst,

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:57.070
I mean, in the throes of
heartache, of pain, of frustration,

00:18:57.070 --> 00:19:00.360
of not understanding where
our next meal might come,

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:02.390
or from being homeless at the moment,

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:04.337
what we have to do is really
focus on the moment and now,

00:19:04.337 --> 00:19:07.070
and then address some of
those specific needs to say,

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:09.050
it's not just words, but also actions

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:11.397
that's gonna allow you to
know somebody to help you.

00:19:11.397 --> 00:19:13.937
And that there are some resources
that you didn't even know.

00:19:13.937 --> 00:19:16.020
And in those ways that you can't help,

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:17.600
it's like what pastor said before.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:19.830
Sometimes it'd be an ear
and a shoulder to say,

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:21.110
the whole world is not cold.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:24.140
Somebody who may be can't
do anything, but will care,

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:28.290
will cry, will share this time
and space of pain with you.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:30.800
- Well, that's so powerful and
showing up in so many ways,

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:33.870
it seems like you're talking
about too, just being there.

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.440
How do people receive that?

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:36.610
'Cause I know there's a whole range

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:39.850
of how people would receive
that, whether they push back

00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:41.530
or you don't understand my situation,

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:42.520
or I'm glad you're here,

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:46.210
how has that component come into play?

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:49.150
- Yeah, it always boils
down to, can you help me?

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.370
And if you can help me,
then obviously it's received

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:53.050
in a little bit different fashion.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.160
If you're only giving words and no action,

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:57.050
then that gets tired after a while.

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:59.380
So what you have to do is
say, what Pastor said before,

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.990
we can't do it all, so how are
you connected with resources?

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:03.660
So even if I can't get you here,

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:05.250
can I get you connected to somebody?

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:08.040
Can I lead you to a way to
say, I know there's some help.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.260
If I know there's no food on the table,

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:13.000
can we bring some food on
that table literally today

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:14.610
and then show you a
way to get it tomorrow?

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:17.270
So you say, how is it received?

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:18.500
How is it accepted?

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:22.050
There's always gonna
be some good, some bad,

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:23.190
but mostly in the middle.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:25.450
It comes to the point
where I am appreciative

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:27.410
that you did what you said, took the time,

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:30.640
did show the care and did
point me into some directions

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.030
or did actually give me
some things that I needed.

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:35.520
That again, gives me back
to that level of hope.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:38.380
Because once you have hope,
you remove the desperation

00:20:38.380 --> 00:20:40.240
because when desperation comes in,

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:41.780
that's when we usually tend to see

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:43.230
the worst of the human being,

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.190
and that's what we wanna try to avoid.

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:47.940
- Yeah, it's so good, it's so powerful.

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:50.360
Pastor Shino, what about you
on some of those topics too?

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:51.660
'Cause you alluded to that,

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:53.360
just with all the calls
you were getting with them,

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:55.840
the devastation, a lot of similar ways

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.180
while the topics may have been different,

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:58.670
people were suffering
at the end of the day.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.340
So how did you reach in and intervene

00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:02.500
and try and do that in a way

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:05.440
that doesn't spread you
too thin at the same time.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:08.050
- For us, it was just networking

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:11.640
and taking the resources that
we had and stretching them out

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:14.360
and getting them to the people
that needed it the most.

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:16.610
And so when COVID hit, first of all,

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:17.870
we're in a multi-ethnic church.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:22.027
So being a man of color
in a multi at the church,

00:21:22.027 --> 00:21:23.580
it was some tough days.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.440
It was some times where
we had to navigate.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:28.780
And it was just like, man,
conversations coming up,

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.930
whether it was through the political times

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:35.330
or the social injustice times,
or it was the economy times,

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:38.850
everything was just like, you
had to be on your Ps and Qs

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.730
because if you said the wrong
thing at the wrong time,

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:43.810
somebody could take it the wrong way

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:46.840
and start pointing fingers
and it just got ugly.

00:21:46.840 --> 00:21:50.450
And so we really needed
to be able to navigate.

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.630
And so for our doctors, for instance,

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:57.250
they were running out of masks
and in different hospitals,

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:00.700
but we have family, church
family, and people over in China

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:02.920
that were able to come up with masks

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:06.447
and get those masks
from China over the us.

00:22:06.447 --> 00:22:08.290
And this is where people of wealth

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:11.140
and means in our church
community were like, look, man,

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.530
just I'll pay for it,
I'll take care of it.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.610
Let us be a blessing to our
doctors, our essential workers.

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:20.480
Just like Dr. Smith was saying,
not just talk, but action.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.510
You know, hey, we struggling over here,

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:26.170
we're the front liners, we're
taking care of this community

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:27.980
and some of our own people.

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:31.670
So let us be, let's not just,
you know, what can we do?

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:33.890
We may not be able to
physically come together,

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:34.723
but what can we do?

00:22:34.723 --> 00:22:37.130
How can we get resources
and things to people?

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:41.430
And that made a major
impact for a lot of doctors.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.430
Doctors to this day, write
our pastors in our church,

00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:47.040
and just say, "Thank
you guys for stepping up

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:49.430
and helping us because we
couldn't wear the same mask."

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:51.110
They were running out a masks.

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:52.920
And so just by the grace of God,

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:55.500
we were able to get
something to these doctors

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:56.480
in different parts.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.180
We're talking Long Island,
we're talking the Bronx,

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:00.670
we're talking just different hospitals,

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.600
spread out through the tri-state region.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:06.760
We're being able to get taken
care of during that time.

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:09.090
So that was life giving.

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:11.170
That was, man, we're doing something

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:14.160
in the midst of one of our darkest moments

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.540
and hours of our time

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:19.920
here in the States and around the world.

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:23.210
So being able to do something like that,

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:25.010
really was a blessing.

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:27.290
Other thing that I thought was important

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:30.173
was during the social injustice times,

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:36.380
it was important for us to
have hard conversations.

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:40.290
Now, even though you may not
be the color that I may be,

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.650
and you may not understand how I feel

00:23:42.650 --> 00:23:46.670
and what I'm going
through during this time,

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:49.700
it's important that people
were willing to learn.

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:53.310
They wanted to know, I mean,
I get it but I need to know.

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:54.760
They were educating themselves,

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:57.170
they're reading books,
they're coming to meetings.

00:23:57.170 --> 00:24:00.530
They're allowing people to
say what needed to be said,

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.710
having uncomfortable conversations.

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:05.570
I mean, conversations that
would make you cringe,

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:07.890
but they're not just
listening, but they're saying,

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.610
now, what can we do, how
can we be a part of this?

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:13.540
So help facilitating those moments,

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:15.630
participating in those moments,

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:19.350
being candid in those
moments was very important

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:21.710
for a lot of people in our communities.

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:23.360
We're talking about the community

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:26.060
of just being in a multi-ethnic community

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:26.893
was very important,

00:24:26.893 --> 00:24:30.283
but being in a multiethnic
church was important as well,

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:35.947
bringing people out to march and to talk

00:24:37.630 --> 00:24:41.010
and people that don't look
like you hand-in-hand,

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:43.100
arm-in-arm brought something.

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:46.440
It shows people stuff
that we may disagree,

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:48.040
but we still love each other.

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:51.020
And the most powerful thing
right now during this time

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:54.720
was that I was preaching love,
and not just preaching it,

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.460
but demonstrating it
'cause love is action.

00:24:57.460 --> 00:24:59.400
Love isn't just talk, love is action.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.070
I got to get out here and
I gotta be with the people.

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:03.830
I got to do stuff with the people.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:08.020
And so it's hard to have people follow you

00:25:08.020 --> 00:25:11.110
if you're not leading, if
you're not leading in that way.

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:12.510
So a lot of people are like, all right,

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:14.440
if you're gonna be out
there, we coming out there,

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:16.790
you know, and we're gonna do these things.

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:21.790
Another thing that was important
for me with the community

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:26.240
was being a sounding
board to police officers.

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:29.840
Now I notice it's gonna
sound awkward or strange,

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:34.500
but as a pastor, I had to get
out there with other people

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:37.100
and hear their story and being able to go

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:41.500
and I was able to talk
to 150 police officers

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:43.170
from all over the country.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.760
These are some current and some former

00:25:45.760 --> 00:25:49.370
and letting them know, I hear
you, I understand your pain,

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:50.310
I know what you're going through.

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:52.000
Yes, you're a father,
yes, you're a husband,

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.400
I mean, yes, you're a grandfather.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.680
Yes, all that, you know,
you're a human being,

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:56.860
put it that way.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.437
And putting myself out there and saying,

00:25:59.437 --> 00:26:02.270
"Thank you for protecting our communities

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:04.000
and all this stuff that needed to do."

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.810
It cut across the
granite society, it hurt.

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.670
Matter of fact, some people
weren't happy about it.

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:12.970
But once again, as a man of God,

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:16.100
I had to step out and do
some uncomfortable things

00:26:16.100 --> 00:26:17.500
to show somebody something.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:19.950
Because at the end of
the day, what I realized,

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:22.920
just where we are today, 2021 of June,

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:24.820
you look, things have lifted,

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:26.520
things are going back to normal.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:29.680
And so I was able to build
some bridges with some people

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:33.730
during those difficult times,
now they're calling this in.

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:36.440
As things are being restored,
things are being repaired,

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:39.350
relationships, friendships,

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:41.180
things are starting to be repaired.

00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:43.400
I thank God, I was able to
build a bridge back then

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:44.920
when the storm was happening,

00:26:44.920 --> 00:26:46.210
instead of trying to build a bridge now.

00:26:46.210 --> 00:26:48.170
Now I have a little relational equity.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:49.490
And I think that what's important

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:52.710
is having a relational
equity during dark moments,

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:56.710
during dark times, you were
there with me at the bottom.

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:59.250
And now that we're starting
to rise up out of the top,

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:01.290
we can build together,

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:04.180
we can do some greater things
together in our communities.

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:06.912
- Mm that's so good, and
again, that theme of community,

00:27:06.912 --> 00:27:08.510
that's kind of across what
you're both talking about.

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:10.330
Just really meeting
people where they're at,

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:12.330
being there, sincerely
being there with action.

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:13.700
I think that goes such a long way

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:17.410
and I'm encouraged to just
see how things move forward

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:18.900
with those bridges being built.

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:20.960
I mean, especially as we
talk about mental health

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:24.250
and just people's awareness
of those types of things,

00:27:24.250 --> 00:27:25.250
the challenge that we face.

00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:26.083
And I think even the way

00:27:26.083 --> 00:27:27.670
that you both shared your stories about,

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:29.210
personally, I didn't
take that time to rest.

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:30.290
I know for me, with my students,

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:32.370
I had to share those stories, honestly.

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:34.460
And there's something
different about as healers,

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:36.120
if we share that ourselves,

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:37.820
it seems like it takes it
to a whole another level

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:39.990
that people can actually
relate to grasp it,

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:41.510
rather than if we just talk about it

00:27:41.510 --> 00:27:44.310
in a kind of abstract way, or
this is what you need to do,

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:46.090
but don't bring ourselves
into that conversation.

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:48.690
So I'm grateful for the ways
that both of you had done that.

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.070
So powerfully in so many different ways.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:54.240
And I'm also curious how you
see things moving forward,

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:56.090
because it seems like at
least in a lot of ways,

00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:58.410
everything we've gone
through is shaken us up,

00:27:58.410 --> 00:27:59.700
woken us up to a lot of things,

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:01.700
but I'm hopeful that it's also woken us up

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:05.690
to just the importance of
emotional wellness too.

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:07.990
Do you think people are catching that

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:09.320
or do you feel like that's something

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:11.180
that's kind of momentary and passing?

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:12.500
If people are catching it, are there ways

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:14.230
that you're trying to make
sure that continues for,

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:17.450
and that people really pay
attention to those components?

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:18.790
So I think in both of what you're doing,

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:20.503
that that's an essential piece.

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.440
- Yeah, I think their more,
I'll let you go first Pastor.

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:33.470
And so, no, I think it's a point.

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:36.120
I look at January 1st, what
happens with everybody?

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:38.690
That's the day of what
resolution, we're gonna do it,

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:39.540
we're gonna go here.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:42.130
So it's always, I think
something innate in us

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:44.970
to wanna restart, to be
better, to improve ourselves.

00:28:44.970 --> 00:28:46.810
I think there's some
things that happen then

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:48.980
that starts to accelerate that clock.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:50.200
You go into the doctor and they say,

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:52.210
oh, you have diabetes, you
have to change your diet.

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:53.940
That will force you to eat differently.

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:55.730
So sometimes it's from negative things,

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:57.930
sometimes it's like, you see
somebody else do something,

00:28:57.930 --> 00:28:58.763
where you'd be like,

00:28:58.763 --> 00:29:00.960
that's what I wanna do
in a proactive manner.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:03.350
Now, when we get something
like COVID, you have to say,

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:05.660
it brings out the best
and the worst of us,

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:07.820
because whenever we're in this
type of situation, it does.

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:10.270
So some people have recognized
that, wow, you know what?

00:29:10.270 --> 00:29:13.100
During this time I had
more time to now work out

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:14.390
to be able to eat different

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:15.900
'cause I wasn't going out as much.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:17.890
I can do A, I can do B, I can do C,

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:19.450
which was all constructive.

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:21.650
But on the other end,
now you have some people

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:24.040
we call it the COVID-19
for another reason,

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:25.980
'cause those 19 piles are put on.

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:28.670
I am a little depressed, I
am not able to sleep right

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:30.780
and these are some things
that contribute to it.

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:32.530
But now from the emotional standpoint,

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:34.360
when we look at the lack of socialization

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:37.910
as so many people had, moving
from face-to-face interaction,

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:38.743
moving to the point

00:29:38.743 --> 00:29:41.360
where we had six feet
of separation literally,

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:43.330
but what that meant to us emotionally.

00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:46.300
So now the question
becomes, how do we recover?

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:47.940
When a person goes into a military,

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:50.370
if you've ever seen like
an 18 year old young man

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:53.110
going into the military, young, immature.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:55.130
Right after bootcamp, it
looks like a different person

00:29:55.130 --> 00:29:57.960
because there's been a training,
it's something intense.

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:00.580
And so what happened is
when we went into COVID,

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:02.690
we were all forced to go
under a boot training,

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:05.520
a boot camp in a different way
and we learned some things.

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:07.040
But now this is the key,

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:09.280
just like a person
who's returning from war

00:30:09.280 --> 00:30:12.160
needs to be retaught, to be
reintegrated into society,

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:15.080
to make sure that the things
that was happening at war,

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:15.913
they can remove that

00:30:15.913 --> 00:30:18.870
and then reintegrate
naturally back into society.

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:20.477
That's what's going on
with so many of us here,

00:30:20.477 --> 00:30:22.480
and this is where the
emotional wellness come.

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:26.830
What will we do to now,
to what rewind our minds,

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:28.890
to go back to that old sense of normal

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:31.800
that we're going into, that
we haven't been in for awhile.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:32.633
What will we do?

00:30:32.633 --> 00:30:35.410
So it's gonna be about how
assertive the person it is.

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:38.390
And then that depends on what
are some of the situations

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:40.520
that we're going through
because let's be honest,

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:42.510
the brighter your outlooks, Pastor said,

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.050
people were losing jobs,
people were losing houses,

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:46.780
was harder to have emotional wellness

00:30:46.780 --> 00:30:48.380
and to take care of some of those things

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:49.710
if you have those pressing needs

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:51.700
from a Maslow's hierarchy of needs

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:53.980
situation going on theory or not.

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:55.780
But when things aren't going good,

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:57.300
you do have those basic needs.

00:30:57.300 --> 00:30:58.640
That's the time where you can say,

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:00.500
I do have the emotional wherewithal

00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:02.690
to really try to like
take good care of myself,

00:31:02.690 --> 00:31:03.990
to be able to do those things.

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:05.990
So I think what we have
to do is to look at people

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:07.930
individual situations,

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:10.530
but then also look at
some of those past traumas

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.010
that could have been re-triggered
from the COVID and say,

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:17.010
how do we properly reintegrate
everybody back into society?

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.310
So for every parent, for
every school teacher,

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:20.690
for every employer,

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:22.840
we're telling them to take a
different look at the people

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:24.710
who are coming back because everybody

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:25.900
is coming on different levels.

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:28.040
And if we try to do a
cookie-cutter approach,

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:30.077
we're gonna lose so many
people in so many ways

00:31:30.077 --> 00:31:31.750
and it's gonna deteriorate.

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:33.340
But if we can catch
people where they're at,

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:34.820
that emotional wellness,

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:36.910
then we can help them
escalate slowly at a pace

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:38.940
that's gonna be helpful and beneficial,

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:41.710
not only for them, but for
the community at large.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:42.740
- Yeah, I think that was so good.

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:43.740
And actually I have a question on that,

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:47.066
'cause I know sometimes when
I'm trying to advise people

00:31:47.066 --> 00:31:48.570
and things like that,
people are feeling surprised

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:50.140
because it's almost like in their minds,

00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:51.530
they're wanting everything to go back

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:53.350
to exactly the way it was.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:56.460
And then because it's not exactly
the way it was that again,

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:58.240
kind of leads them into
a tailspin of anxiety,

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:00.450
because then they're
not sure how to navigate

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:01.640
this new normal in a sense.

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:02.853
There's still even this people

00:32:02.853 --> 00:32:04.920
that are in certain areas of the country

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:06.870
the masks aren't as prevalent,

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:08.840
then some people are feeling
really uncomfortable with that.

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:09.960
Some people might be more septal.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:12.040
So how do you meet people individually

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:15.700
with those types of things to
kind of set, well, I guess,

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:17.440
realistic expectations in a way

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:20.140
that doesn't lead people
astray when they, again,

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:22.434
hit things that aren't what they expected.

00:32:22.434 --> 00:32:25.160
- Know, I think it's to the
point where we just literally,

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:26.606
we asked the questions.

00:32:26.606 --> 00:32:29.150
When we come in, we can't just start off,

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:32.000
like you said, as is, we have
to take a moment to pause

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:35.040
and say, listen, we need to
reset, we need to recalculate,

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.260
we need to recalibrate
this whole thing and say,

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:38.093
where are we at?

00:32:38.093 --> 00:32:40.750
Just to the point who feels
with Madison, who doesn't?

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:42.840
Because now you can kind
of create certain groups.

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:44.440
You can say, this is what we're gonna do

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:45.970
to put you where you're comfortable.

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:47.980
But the thing is, if we don't ask,

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:49.480
we don't know if we make assumptions,

00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:51.200
then we put people in categories

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:54.120
that probably won't be
to their best interest.

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:55.210
And Pastor, I apologize.

00:32:55.210 --> 00:32:56.363
I thought you was about to say something,

00:32:56.363 --> 00:32:59.970
I shouldn't need to cut
you off, 1000 apologies.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:03.930
- No, no, no, it's everything
you're saying, I echo

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.694
because it's funny where
we're trying to regather

00:33:06.694 --> 00:33:07.944
and we're coming back in,

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:11.970
the church is starting
to come back together.

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:15.510
Now some people chose to do it anyway,

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:18.700
but we, something that
Dr. Smith was saying,

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:21.440
meeting people where they're at.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:25.370
And one thing we did is
'cause everybody is something,

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:29.580
it was mask, no mask, social
distance, no social...

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:30.960
People just didn't,

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:32.980
all they knew is they just
wanted to get back together,

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:34.660
but there was some people that didn't.

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:37.870
And so we had to take the assessment,

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:41.240
we had to put the survey
out and you would be amazed

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:43.076
at what some people were
saying on the survey.

00:33:43.076 --> 00:33:46.520
And so we said, okay,
collectively, what we have to do

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:49.260
is show people that we're using wisdom.

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:52.030
We're not just gonna
do this thing blindly,

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:53.710
but we're gonna do this in stages

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:55.490
and we're gonna do it right.

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:58.610
So we got our doctors, in our house,

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:01.630
that they get paid a lot of
money to do what they do.

00:34:01.630 --> 00:34:03.590
And they are doctors,
they're professionals,

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:05.170
they got all the alphabets behind the...

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:07.745
so that made us look good,

00:34:07.745 --> 00:34:10.390
And we said, "What should we
do, what are you guys hearing?"

00:34:10.390 --> 00:34:13.440
And began to ask them questions,

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:17.460
and they begin to lay it out
exactly what Dr. Smith said.

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:19.500
There are people that are
gonna be here mentally,

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:20.910
there are people that are scared

00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:22.750
or people that are frightened,

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:25.260
or then you got the gung-ho
people that just don't care,

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:28.700
they're just ready to re-emerge
and get back to normal.

00:34:28.700 --> 00:34:32.780
And so we've had to balance
that and meet people halfway

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:35.600
and say, okay, if you wanna wear a mask,

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:37.510
we're not gonna shun you about that,

00:34:37.510 --> 00:34:39.800
do what's best for you and your family.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:42.280
But if somebody over here chooses not to,

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:45.000
then that's up to them.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:48.050
But just meeting people where they are

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:51.850
and not putting that extra
pressure or extra stress on them

00:34:51.850 --> 00:34:54.400
to make them try to do
something or be something

00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:56.480
that they're not ready to do or be

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:58.740
in this season of their life.

00:34:58.740 --> 00:35:02.250
One thing I've learned a
long time ago, slow is fast,

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:04.153
and I would rather do
something the right way

00:35:04.153 --> 00:35:07.170
than do something the wrong
way and then it blows up.

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:08.950
And so people are frustrated.

00:35:08.950 --> 00:35:12.090
You guys should be meeting,
you're letting this happen.

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:13.874
And I'm like, no, no,

00:35:13.874 --> 00:35:17.340
we'll come back together when it's right.

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:20.710
And then fortunately
it's starting to happen.

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:24.370
And so meeting people where
they are, listening to them,

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:28.450
being mindful of children, I
think a lot of times as adults,

00:35:28.450 --> 00:35:31.340
we forget that, we forget where they are

00:35:31.340 --> 00:35:33.217
and what they're dealing with.

00:35:33.217 --> 00:35:34.177
And if we're going through it,

00:35:34.177 --> 00:35:37.230
and then they probably going
through it twice as bad.

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:41.360
And so being able to stop
and say, but what about them?

00:35:41.360 --> 00:35:44.520
You know, they're the priority right now.

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:47.520
So we have to make sure
that they're healthy,

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:50.220
that they're whole, that
they're being talked to

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:51.280
and loved and cared for,

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:53.067
just as much as we're trying to get it.

00:35:53.067 --> 00:35:57.110
And so that was a big deal
for us is just saying,

00:35:57.110 --> 00:35:59.140
we got to take care of our babies.

00:35:59.140 --> 00:36:01.160
We can't be worrying
about like what we want,

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:02.810
but we need to be at
a pace where they are,

00:36:02.810 --> 00:36:06.030
because this is just not normal for them.

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:09.270
It's just not going to
school and things like that.

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:14.270
So that was a big thing
for us and for our people,

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:17.180
but meeting people where they are

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:18.540
and not marginalizing them,

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:21.530
or making them feel less than
based off of their emotions

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:24.110
and their feelings or
their mental awareness

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:25.460
was very important.

00:36:25.460 --> 00:36:27.540
- Yeah, that's so good and
it seems like a lot of ways

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:30.550
you are giving people space
and time to just process

00:36:30.550 --> 00:36:32.690
and go through what was happening.

00:36:32.690 --> 00:36:34.140
And so, I think, Dr. Smith,

00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:35.080
you can probably speak to this as well,

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.670
but I think sometimes it's easy for us

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:39.290
to forget about that
with the children too,

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.170
because they're the way they
react is gonna be different

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:44.020
from the way the adults
react, as you mentioned.

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:46.750
And sometimes if we're not
keen to that or realizing that,

00:36:46.750 --> 00:36:48.160
oh, so-and-so is not just acting out,

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:50.010
they're really just trying
to process everything

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:52.100
that's going on, they're
feeding off of the adults too,

00:36:52.100 --> 00:36:54.470
and really just taking the
time to be attended to.

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:55.760
And Pastor Shino as you mentioned,

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:59.240
giving people time to kind of
walk through those pieces too.

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:04.240
'Cause I think sometimes there's
an inclination to go fast,

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:05.503
like you said too.

00:37:05.503 --> 00:37:06.970
Just kind of plow through the emotions,

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:08.690
push it down and ignore it.

00:37:08.690 --> 00:37:10.060
I mean, I've definitely seen people

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:12.310
doing that around anxiety
during the pandemic

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:13.870
or anxiety after it.

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:14.820
It's almost that people are saying,

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:16.980
well, this shouldn't be
as much of a struggle.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.440
So they kind of minimize it, stuff it down

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:20.802
and it just keeps re popping up

00:37:20.802 --> 00:37:23.200
and it just kind of cycles
and cycles and cycles.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:25.170
And I'm curious with both of
you, even elaborating that,

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:28.930
how have you given people
space and time to process that,

00:37:28.930 --> 00:37:30.000
are there specific ways

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.120
that you think it's
helpful in the healthy,

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:36.360
is it an awareness piece,
is it practical tips?

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:39.360
How have you approached that,
Dr. Smith's you wanna jump in?

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:40.993
Oh, no, I'm gonna switch back.

00:37:42.914 --> 00:37:47.040
- No, I was just like,
oh, I was saying for me,

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:52.530
it was creating space
for our men to be men.

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:57.530
I think a lot of times, you're
looked at weak or whatever,

00:37:57.700 --> 00:38:00.440
if you're losing it, you
know what I'm saying?

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:01.450
If you're really losing it.

00:38:01.450 --> 00:38:03.930
And I recognize and realize

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:06.880
based off of what was
coming across my screen

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:07.990
guys were checking out.

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:09.576
You know, when I say checking out,

00:38:09.576 --> 00:38:11.730
some brothers was like leaving,

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:14.480
like, I'm out of here, I'm gone.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:18.725
And it was like, whoa, you
know, we have to address this.

00:38:18.725 --> 00:38:23.725
And so creating spaces
where men could be men

00:38:24.070 --> 00:38:26.370
and talk about what's really going on

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:32.140
being leaders in their homes
and it was a safe place.

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:34.810
It was safe, but bringing
in the right people,

00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:37.520
so brought in a Dr. Suite to talk to them.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:39.820
We brought in a Dr. Nii
Addy to talk to them.

00:38:39.820 --> 00:38:43.720
We brought in a Dr. Schaefer,
men that could minister,

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:46.000
not just from the Word of God,

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:49.470
but also from a clinical
point of view as well,

00:38:49.470 --> 00:38:53.440
meeting both the body and the
soul because men were broken,

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:55.400
for the first time real pressure,

00:38:55.400 --> 00:38:57.640
they couldn't pull them
stress out of the bootstraps.

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:02.600
You know, you couldn't,
whatever faith through it,

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:04.390
you needed some real help.

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:07.020
And having guys being
able to come together

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:10.414
and say, man, this is
what's going on in my home.

00:39:10.414 --> 00:39:13.280
This is what's going on on my job,

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:15.780
or this is what's going
on with my children.

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:19.850
And hearing everybody across
the board say, amen, amen.

00:39:19.850 --> 00:39:22.140
That's what's happening in my house too.

00:39:22.140 --> 00:39:25.147
How can we do this, how
can we navigate this?

00:39:25.147 --> 00:39:28.560
And with that said,
what it did for the men,

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:32.360
what it made them realize
that they're not alone

00:39:32.360 --> 00:39:35.520
and that they're not the
only ones going through it.

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:36.720
So it made them feel like,

00:39:36.720 --> 00:39:39.770
hey man, I'm not losing
it and I'm not crazy.

00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:41.550
And so it was really important for them

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:42.670
to be able to share that.

00:39:42.670 --> 00:39:47.060
And I mean guys were
crying, guys were weeping,

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:50.110
and dudes were on the verge of the,

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:50.943
I'm just gonna be honest,

00:39:50.943 --> 00:39:52.850
they were on the verge of divorce, man,

00:39:52.850 --> 00:39:55.337
because they just, they
couldn't handle it.

00:39:55.337 --> 00:39:59.160
The stress that was going on in the home,

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:03.000
the pressures of having to make
it happen at the workplace,

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:06.307
but not being able to go to
work and all those things.

00:40:06.307 --> 00:40:10.480
And so man, being able
to create safe places

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:13.510
for guys to be, just
men to be men, to weep,

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:14.900
because we say it like this,

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:17.740
as the men go, so goes to society.

00:40:17.740 --> 00:40:20.710
And I mean, I'm by no
means in my marginalizing

00:40:20.710 --> 00:40:22.720
or putting down women
or anything like that.

00:40:22.720 --> 00:40:26.650
But what I am saying is
the men are, you know,

00:40:26.650 --> 00:40:29.900
they wanna come in and they
wanna provide and protect

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:32.780
and do all the things
that they are wired to do.

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:34.690
And for the first time,

00:40:34.690 --> 00:40:36.810
it didn't feel like they could do that.

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:38.470
Things are being taken away from them

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:40.920
and it was this important for them

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:43.060
to be able to come into safe places,

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:45.790
to talk and discuss and
be edified and built up

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:49.210
and get real solutions to real problems.

00:40:49.210 --> 00:40:52.040
- That's so good, Dr. Smith,
you wanna jump in as well?

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:54.310
- Yeah, you know, Pastor
Shino said the magic word,

00:40:54.310 --> 00:40:55.460
you know, it's normalcy,

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:57.470
'cause everybody's
saying, oh, it's just me.

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:00.230
Once you get that idea,
okay, it's not just me,

00:41:00.230 --> 00:41:02.160
this everybody's going
through it, guess what?

00:41:02.160 --> 00:41:03.670
Now we can kind of come together.

00:41:03.670 --> 00:41:05.200
It's like, I can get through this

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:07.630
because this is something
everybody is going through.

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:08.900
And so when we talk about that,

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:10.940
we also talked about like those stages

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:13.680
and Pastor Shino talked about
as far as elevating people.

00:41:13.680 --> 00:41:14.960
When we talk about children,

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:16.640
you think about what
did they do in schools?

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:18.970
They went from virtual to hybrid

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:20.840
and then to like fall back in,

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:23.570
because what you gotta do
is slowly give a process

00:41:23.570 --> 00:41:24.403
and a progress.

00:41:24.403 --> 00:41:25.460
But what we have to remember is this,

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:28.930
'cause this is to your
original point, Dr. Addy,

00:41:28.930 --> 00:41:30.670
children brains are not fully developed.

00:41:30.670 --> 00:41:33.520
We know from neuroscience,
the brain does a fully develop

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:35.420
till you're between 25-28.

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:37.920
So that's the prefrontal lobe,
the executive functioning,

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:40.480
the problem solving, the impulse control.

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:42.390
So when we're talking
about younger people,

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:44.600
we can't expect them to think like adults.

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:45.982
So what we do have to do

00:41:45.982 --> 00:41:47.990
is have to have an extra level of grace,

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:50.930
extra level of patience
extra level of understanding,

00:41:50.930 --> 00:41:52.490
and we have to accommodate our rules.

00:41:52.490 --> 00:41:55.480
So any school that has a 0% tolerance,

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:56.790
we're gonna have to relax on that,

00:41:56.790 --> 00:41:58.030
we're going to have to flex on that.

00:41:58.030 --> 00:41:59.960
Now, does that mean we
excuse bad behaviors?

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.610
No, but what we do is we
look at any type of behavior

00:42:02.610 --> 00:42:05.040
that we might consider
to be bad, antisocial

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:07.500
that goes against the rule
as a learning experiment.

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:08.510
And we have to say,

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:10.310
how do we now make
these teachable moments?

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:12.810
How do we, instead of
punishing individuals,

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:15.090
how do we learn and we grow from it?

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:16.510
So now what we have to expect

00:42:16.510 --> 00:42:19.100
is there are gonna be
individual situations

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:22.770
where if we can't express
ourselves verbally, what do we do?

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:24.240
We express ourselves physically.

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:25.290
So we also have to make sure

00:42:25.290 --> 00:42:26.640
that we're creating an environment

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:29.660
that's conducive for safety so
we can minimize these things.

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:31.940
What we have to do is to be
able to see them in advance

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:34.820
so we can nip them in the
bud before they flourish.

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:35.653
And those are the things

00:42:35.653 --> 00:42:37.670
that we don't want to come to fruition.

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:38.840
So I think the thing is,

00:42:38.840 --> 00:42:41.053
how can we be proactive
in our plans to saying,

00:42:41.053 --> 00:42:44.660
what can we predict
based on other societies?

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:47.310
I always laugh from a pop
culture, kind of tells my age,

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:49.410
but remember the story of life.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:52.290
And when Martin Lawrence was
out looking for the first time,

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:55.070
and he'd like, what the world is going on?

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:56.977
What we have to do is give
people a chance to what?

00:42:56.977 --> 00:42:59.940
Assimilate back into society slowly

